    "referenceAnswerEn": "The fundamental difference: utilitarianism locates rightness in consequences. An action is right according to the overall benefit it produces, usually understood as total happiness minus total suffering; actions have no intrinsic moral character, and their moral quality is settled entirely by outcome. Deontology locates rightness in the nature of the act itself. Certain acts are wrong not because their consequences are bad but because they violate a duty or principle — lying, or treating a person merely as a means — and remain wrong even where the consequences are good.\nIn a sentence: utilitarianism looks forward and asks what this will produce; deontology looks inward and asks what kind of act this is.\n\nCommon criticisms: utilitarianism may permit sacrificing a few for the benefit of the many, since if the calculation favours harming one innocent person it is hard to explain why this should not be done, which conflicts with most people’s moral intuitions. Deontology may require adherence to a rule in extreme situations at obvious cost — classically, refusing to lie even where a lie would save an innocent life — and critics hold that moral judgement wholly indifferent to consequences cannot be sustained in practice.",
    "contentEn": "Explain the fundamental difference between utilitarianism and deontology in what they take to determine the rightness of an action, and state one criticism commonly made of each.",
    "explanationEn": "The marks concentrate on the word \"fundamental\". An answer that describes the two theories separately without naming where they diverge has answered two questions but not this one. The most effective move is to compress the divergence into one contrast — one looks at consequences, the other at the act itself — which then gives the rest of the answer a frame.\nA criticism must attach to a structural weakness rather than express general disapproval. \"Utilitarianism is callous\" is not a criticism, since it does not identify which part of the theory produces that result; \"because it aggregates, it can in principle license sacrificing a few\" is, because it points at the method of calculation.\nNote also that raising a criticism is not rejecting the theory. Papers frequently follow up by asking how the theory might reply, so a neutral register serves better than taking sides early."
