    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) A utilitarian assigns the machine where overall benefit is greatest, weighing prospects of recovery, expected years of life and later contribution to others. The surgeon might treat many more patients; the student has better prospects and more expected years. The conclusion turns on the actual figures, not on either identity as such.\nA deontologist refuses usefulness as the basis of allocation, on the ground of intrinsic worth: each person has a dignity that cannot be reduced, and ranking by contribution treats people as instruments. A deontologist is likelier to favour medically relevant criteria — prospect of recovery, urgency — or, where these are comparable, a random allocation, so that no ranking of persons is made.\n\n(b) The calculation requires data that cannot be obtained: how many patients the surgeon would in fact treat, and what the student would contribute, are unknowable futures, and recovery estimates carry real uncertainty. The deeper difficulty is incommensurability — how are \"patients a surgeon might later save\" and \"forty additional years of a student’s life\" to be placed on one scale? They are not the same kind of value, yet the calculation presupposes a common unit. This is a theoretical difficulty, not merely missing information.\n\n(c) Advantage: equality. A lottery does not prejudge whose life is worth more, avoiding ranking by status, occupation or potential contribution, and where neither case is clearly stronger it gives both an equal chance through a transparent procedure.\nDisadvantage: it discards relevant information. If one patient’s prospects are markedly better, a lottery permits the machine to go to a likely futile use, and the cost is borne by the patient who could have been saved. Pure randomisation conflates refusing improper distinctions with refusing any distinction.\n\n(d) The case is a genuine dilemma: two independently valid moral demands cannot both be met, so whichever is chosen, a real moral value is sacrificed and no costless option exists — if one did, the situation would not be a dilemma.\nWhat ethical discussion contributes: it makes the grounds of the choice explicit, so the decision-maker knows what is being sacrificed and why rather than choosing unreflectively; it separates acceptable from unacceptable grounds, ruling out allocation by social standing or ability to pay even where it cannot identify a single right answer; and it makes the decision reviewable, since a decision with stated reasons can be challenged, defended and revised, while one made on instinct cannot be discussed at all. Its role is not to supply the answer but to raise the quality of the choice.",
