    "explanationEn": "The key in (b) is distinguishing two kinds of difficulty. Missing data is a practical problem that better estimates could in principle improve; incommensurability is theoretical — even with complete information, two values of different kinds cannot be reduced to one unit. Reaching the second is where the higher marks lie.\nThe disadvantage in (c) is easily blurred. The trouble with a lottery is not that it is irrational but that it erases medically relevant differences along with improper ones; the sentence about conflating the two refusals is the point.\nPart (d) looks open but has definite requirements: state the structure of the dilemma first, then locate the contribution of ethics in the quality of the choice rather than in supplying an answer. \"Everyone has different values, so there is no standard answer\" slides into relativism and loses the mark — a dilemma lacks a single answer not because there is no moral standard but precisely because more than one holds at once.",
    "markingScheme": "（a）4 分 —— 效益主義的立場連推理依據（2，須提及後果比較而非只寫「選較有用的一方」）；義務論的立場連推理依據（2，須提及人的內在價值或不可僅作手段）。\n（b）2 分 —— 指出資料不可知的實踐困難（1）；指出不可通約的理論困難（1）。只答前者最多 1 分。\n（c）2 分 —— 優點須扣回平等或避免為人排序（1）；缺點須指出抹去了醫療上相關的差異（1）。答「抽籤不公平」而無說明不給分。\n（d）2 分 —— 說明兩難的結構，兩項道德要求不能同時滿足（1）；指出倫理學的作用在於使理由明確、排除不可接受的依據或令決定可被檢視（1）。以相對主義作結者此項不給分。\n本題所列分數為本平台練習用尺度，並非香港考試及評核局的分數分配。參考答案只列必達步驟與判準，刻意不提供可直接抄錄的完整答卷。交卷後請逐項對照自評，機器不會為本題評分。",
    "markingSchemeEn": "(a) 4 marks — the utilitarian position with its reasoning (2, requiring comparison of consequences rather than \"pick the more useful one\"); the deontological position with its reasoning (2, requiring intrinsic worth or the means-only formulation).\n(b) 2 marks — the practical difficulty of unknowable data (1); the theoretical difficulty of incommensurability (1). The first alone caps at 1.\n(c) 2 marks — an advantage tied to equality or avoiding the ranking of persons (1); a disadvantage identifying the erasure of medically relevant differences (1). \"A lottery is unfair\" without explanation earns nothing.\n(d) 2 marks — the structure of the dilemma, two demands that cannot both be met (1); the contribution of ethics in making grounds explicit, excluding unacceptable grounds, or making the decision reviewable (1). A relativist conclusion earns nothing here.\nThe marks shown are a practice scale used on this platform, not an HKEAA mark allocation. The reference answer lists the required steps and criteria rather than a copyable model script. Compare your own work against it point by point; nothing here is machine-marked.",
